  "slug": "optical-assisted-electro-2157",
  "summary": "Recent preprints demonstrate resonant light control in cryogenic silicon, gain-assisted fiber and modulated optomechanical platforms.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of resonant cavities to shape coherent light through nonlinear or modulated interactions. A passive silicon microring at 4.5 K sustains telecom-band optical parametric oscillation under a single continuous-wave pump, with a reported 2.25 mW onset. Two gain-assisted electro-optic fiber ring resonators with intracavity modulation produce a 1.8 THz flat-top comb for dual-comb spectroscopy. Intensity-modulated driving near mechanical resonance extends optomechanical oscillation amplitude and comb bandwidth toward the cavity-bandwidth limit. A thin-film lithium-niobate oscillator reports a roughly 100-fold enhanced thermal frequency coefficient, while a 60-cm in-vacuum Zerodur cavity addresses atom interferometry stability rather than light generation. What is new is the demonstration of these processes in specific platforms: cryogenic passive silicon, gain-assisted electro-optic fiber cavities, and modulated optomechanical systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
